---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="About — KR4OGH pico balloons"
  description="How the KR4OGH pico balloon program works: WSPR telemetry, Traquito trackers, and a three-store data architecture."
>
  <div class="about prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      A pico balloon is a foil party balloon carrying a solar-powered radio
      tracker that weighs a few grams. Filled with just enough lift, it floats
      in the stratosphere and goes wherever the wind does. There is no
      recovery and no control, so radio is the only thing that comes back.
    </p>
    <p>
      KR4OGH flies stock <a href="https://traquito.github.io/">Traquito</a>
      trackers on the 20&nbsp;m band using
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WSPR_(amateur_radio_software)">WSPR</a>,
      a mode built for very weak signals. Volunteer receiving stations decode
      the transmissions and report them to
      <a href="https://wspr.live/">wspr.live</a>. Position, altitude, speed,
      voltage and processor temperature are packed into pairs of WSPR messages
      using
      <a href="https://qrp-labs.com/u4b/u4bdecoding.html">U4B channelised telemetry</a>;
      the <a href="https://github.com/ryanmio/kr4ogh-pico">code behind this site</a>
      decodes them.
    </p>

    <h2>Flight one</h2>
    <p>
      Two trackers, <strong>F1A</strong> on channel 206 and
      <strong>F1B</strong> on channel 348, go up the same morning as one
      experiment: same callsign and frequency, different time slots. Launches
      are not announced in advance, so flights show up here once they are
      airborne and reporting.
    </p>

    <h2>Scoring</h2>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Pass</th>
          <td>Floats at 8,000&nbsp;m or higher, valid positions spanning at
            least 48 hours, and still transmitting after the first sunrise.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Fail</th>
          <td>No signal in the first 12 hours, or transmits on day one and
            never again after the first sunrise.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Wounded</th>
          <td>A reporting gap over 24 hours, presumed alive; the flight
            continues.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Closed</th>
          <td>Silence for more than 7 days.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      A gap overnight is not a failure. The tracker has no battery and stops
      transmitting when the sun goes down.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the data lives</h2>
    <p>Three stores, with different jobs:</p>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>SQLite</th>
          <td>The flight record: raw spots, query coverage, decoded
            telemetry. Local, and works with no network or credentials.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Supabase</th>
          <td>A cache of decoded telemetry that this site reads while a flight
            is up. The tool only writes to it, and it can pause or break
            without affecting the record.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>git</th>
          <td>The archive. When a flight ends, its track and write-up are
            committed to the repo, and its page here is built from those files
            with no database involved.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The decoder, channel table, ingest tool, this site and the archived
      tracks are all at
      <a href="https://github.com/ryanmio/kr4ogh-pico">github.com/ryanmio/kr4ogh-pico</a>,
      with the wire format and decode validation written up under
      <code>docs/</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
